22:37:07,685 root DEBUG {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You will be given a SQL query and some strategies to rewrite the given SQL query. Your task is to summarize the provided query rewrite strategies into one paragraph.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\nSQL Query:\n```sql\nselect \n   s_store_name\n  ,s_company_id\n  ,s_street_number\n  ,s_street_name\n  ,s_street_type\n  ,s_suite_number\n  ,s_city\n  ,s_county\n  ,s_state\n  ,s_zip\n  ,sum(case when (sr_returned_date_sk - ss_sold_date_sk <= 30 ) then 1 else 0 end)  as "30 days"\n  ,sum(case when (sr_returned_date_sk - ss_sold_date_sk > 30) and\n                 (sr_returned_date_sk - ss_sold_date_sk <= 60) then 1 else 0 end )  as "31-60 days"\n  ,sum(case when (sr_returned_date_sk - ss_sold_date_sk > 60) and\n                 (sr_returned_date_sk - ss_sold_date_sk <= 90) then 1 else 0 end)  as "61-90 days"\n  ,sum(case when (sr_returned_date_sk - ss_sold_date_sk > 90) and\n                 (sr_returned_date_sk - ss_sold_date_sk <= 120) then 1 else 0 end)  as "91-120 days"\n  ,sum(case when (sr_returned_date_sk - ss_sold_date_sk  > 120) then 1 else 0 end)  as ">120 days"\nfrom\n   store_sales\n  ,store_returns\n  ,store\n  ,date_dim d1\n  ,date_dim d2\nwhere\n    d2.d_year = 2000\nand d2.d_moy  = 7\nand ss_ticket_number = sr_ticket_number\nand ss_item_sk = sr_item_sk\nand ss_sold_date_sk   = d1.d_date_sk\nand sr_returned_date_sk   = d2.d_date_sk\nand ss_customer_sk = sr_customer_sk\nand ss_store_sk = s_store_sk\nand d1.d_date between (d2.d_date - interval \'120\' day)\n               and d2.d_date\ngroup by\n   s_store_name\n  ,s_company_id\n  ,s_street_number\n  ,s_street_name\n  ,s_street_type\n  ,s_suite_number\n  ,s_city\n  ,s_county\n  ,s_state\n  ,s_zip\norder by s_store_name\n        ,s_company_id\n        ,s_street_number\n        ,s_street_name\n        ,s_street_type\n        ,s_suite_number\n        ,s_city\n        ,s_county\n        ,s_state\n        ,s_zip\nlimit 100;\n```\n\nQuery Rewrite Strategies:\nQuery Rewrite 1:\n"""The query rewrite process involves moving the filter conditions `d2.d_year = 2000` and `d2.d_moy = 7` into the ON clause of the first LogicalJoin operation. This is done to reduce the size of the datasets before the join occurs, which can improve query performance. The rewritten query will produce the same result as the original query, but with improved efficiency."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 2:\n"""The query can be optimized by rearranging the sequence of operations to prioritize grouping. This can be achieved by moving the `GROUP BY` clause to an earlier stage in the execution process, ideally before executing operations like `JOIN`. This will reduce the size of intermediate datasets and minimize the workload on subsequent operations, leading to performance improvements."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 3:\n"""The query can be optimized by combining `LIMIT` with `ORDER BY`. This will encourage the database engine to stop sorting as soon as the required number of rows is obtained, avoiding full table sorts. Additionally, using `DISTINCT` along with `LIMIT` will lead to an early termination of the search for unique rows, reducing time and resources spent on scanning the entire dataset."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 4:\n"""The query can be optimized by moving repeated function calls or operations outside of loops. For example, if a deterministic function is being called in a loop, calculate the value once before the loop and store the result for reuse. Additionally, replace inline functions with pre-calculated columns or use subqueries to reduce the number of times the function is executed."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 5:\n"""The query can be optimized by combining multiple joins into a single join operation using `CASE` statements. This will reduce redundant data processing and complexity, leading to improved performance and simplified query maintenance."""'}], 'response': "Here's a summary of the provided SQL query rewrite strategies in one paragraph:\n\nTo optimize the given SQL query, several strategies can be employed. Firstly, filter conditions can be moved into the ON clause of the first LogicalJoin operation (Query Rewrite 1) to reduce dataset sizes before joining. Secondly, the sequence of operations can be rearranged to prioritize grouping (Query Rewrite 2), reducing intermediate dataset sizes and workload on subsequent operations. Additionally, combining LIMIT with ORDER BY (Query Rewrite 3) will encourage early termination of sorting, while using DISTINCT along with LIMIT will reduce scanning time. Furthermore, repeated function calls or operations can be moved outside loops (Query Rewrite 4) to reduce execution frequency, and multiple joins can be combined into a single join operation using CASE statements (Query Rewrite 5) to simplify query maintenance and improve performance.", 'time': 1.758218765258789}
